### Spectral analysis

During the third _IXPE_ observation coordinated _Swift_-XRT and NICER observations were also available (see Sect. 2) and provided additional spectral coverage in the soft X-ray band. Therefore, we adopted the same spectral model introduced by Diaz Trigo et al. (2012) to model _XMM-Newton_ data from GX 13+1. In the spectral and spectropolarimetric analyses, we used xspec v.12.14.0 (Arnaud, 1996). The continuum was modeled by the sum of a soft multicolor disk, _i.e._ diskbb (Mitsuda et al., 1984), describing the emission from the inner disk and/or the NS surface, and a harder Comptonized emission in an optically thick environment such as SL/BL, approximated here with bbodyrad. Absorption was modeled with TBabs, which is based on the abundances reported in Wilms et al. (2000). Our overall continuum model was thus tbabs*(diskbb+bbodyrad). The two _Swift_-XRT observations, which were both away from the dips, showed consistent spectra, with best-fit parameters also in agreement with those reported in Bobrikova et al. (2024) (see Table 2).

The NICER data partially covered the _IXPE_ observation with several snapshots, some of which were during dips (see Table 1 and Figure 1). To model the spectra in the Dip and Off-dip states, the data were selected on the basis of their hardness ratio (HR), as shown in Figure 1. By virtue of its better spectral capabilities and throughput (compared to _IXPE_ and _Swift_-XRT) NICER resolved some of the absorption features reported in the literature (Diaz Trigo et al., 2012; D'Ai et al., 2014; Saavedra et al., 2023). In particular, residuals in the 6-8 keV region were clearly visible when the continuum model was fitted to the spectra. To account for those features, we followed the approach in Diaz Trigo et al. (2012) and included a warmabs component to model absorption by photoionized plasma along the line of sight. Moreover, a cabs component was included to account for the effects of Thomson/Compton scattering, which was found to be significant when modeling both the persistent and dipping emission (Boirin et al., 2005; Diaz Trigo et al., 2006, 2012). The latter component was forced to have \\times 1.21 the column density of the warmabs component (which accounts for the number of electrons per hydrogen atom for solar abundances material Stelzer et al., 1999). Furthermore, a gaussian component was included to account for a broad Fe line emission. The complete model we fit to the NICER spectra was thus TBabs*warmabs*cabs*(diskbb+bbodyrad+gauss). The best-fit results reported in Table 3 are in general agreement with those from _XMM-Newton_ and _Chandra_ observations (Diaz Trigo et al., 2012; D'Ai et al., 2014). The spectra are shown in Figure 6. Absorption was higher during the dips, especially during the first one, whereas the diskbb temperature was lower, \\sim0.5 keV, than outside the dips (\\sim1 keV). The bbodyrad component remained instead at a nearly constant temperature of \\sim1.1-1.2 keV.

