As a first attempt, we assumed the same constant polarization for diskbb+bbodyrad and no polarization for the Gaussian (see, e.g., Churazov et al., 2002): tbabs*(polconst*(diskbb+bbodyrad)+gauss). The best-fit from the Dip data gave3 PD = 2.5\\%\\pm 0.7\\% and PA = -37^{\\circ}\\pm 8^{\\circ} (\\chi^{2}/d.o.f. = 441/433). For the Off-dip data the best-fit yielded PD = 1.2\\%\\pm 0.3\\% with PA = -7^{\\circ}\\pm 7^{\\circ} (\\chi^{2}/d.o.f. = 469/433). These results parallel those obtained from the model-independent analysis: during the dips, when absorption was higher, the degree of polarization was higher, and the polarization angle was different with respect to the Off-dip state.

Footnote 3: The other free parameters in the fit — such as the cross-normalizations, norm of diskbb, bbodyrad and gaussian — in both Dip and Off-dip cases have values compatible with the ones reported in Table 4.

As a second step, we associated separate polconst polarization components to the diskbb and bbodyrad components: tbabs*(polconst*diskbb+polconst*bbodyrad+gauss). The best-fit results for this model are reported in Table 4. Polarization was detected at a level of PD\\sim4%across the Off-dip and Dip states in the harder component (bbodyrad), together with marginal evidence for a PA change of \\sim 20^{\\circ}. For the diskbb component we found PD = 10\\%\\pm 4\\% in the Dip state, whereas in the Off-dip state we derived an upper limit of PD<4.3% at 90% CL. We warn that the soft component's 10% PD during the dip should be considered with care, as the polarizations of the two components in the fit result are almost orthogonal, meaning that they subtract from each other and partial degeneracy sets in.

